  "summary_text": "Kyle, yesterday was a mixed picture with a bright spot you should own: Hicksville ran hot, and Port Washington's retail surge masked a grooming capacity crunch that needs attention today.\n\n**The win:** Hicksville delivered $507 per groomer against a $427 trailing baseline \u2014 that's 19% above norm and DOW-adjusted MTD is +19.2% versus expectation. Core groom revenue jumped 58% week-over-week, add-on attach improved to 65%, and appointment volume jumped 30%. This is execution at scale on a two-location platform. Port Washington's retail also spiked 50% despite grooming softness, so your front-of-house team found margin where they could.\n\n**The concern:** Port Washington grooming revenue dropped 27% yesterday on only 4 groomers producing $426 per head \u2014 10% below your trailing baseline. This wasn't a productivity miss; it was a capacity miss. Core groom revenue fell $690 week-over-week while add-ons actually held discipline (58% attach, up from 52%). You're running thin. Week-to-date appointments are down 23% and the calendar hasn't changed \u2014 this is coverage-driven. The good news: MTD Port Washington is still +8% actual versus +4.4% DOW-adjusted expectation, so you're outperforming on the month. But Saturday's gap signals you need groomer hours back in the schedule.\n\n**Operational pattern:** Both locations show you're hitting your lapse pool hard \u2014 Port Washington has 469 dormant/lapsed customers worth $296K annual, Hicksville 119 worth $91K. That's reactivation upside if you can find grooming capacity to service it.\n\n**Capacity & coverage:** Today: 1 groomer scheduled at Port Washington, 0 at Hicksville (Sunday skeleton crew, expected); tomorrow 6 + 1 groomer-days; next 7 days 38 groomer-days total, 43 bather hours \u2014 but that's back-loaded; you're exposed today and tomorrow."
